The homepage sold a finished floor with pictures of a finished floor.
That is the one part of the job every competitor can photograph too, and
the one part a buyer cannot evaluate: every sanded floor looks good on
the day it is handed over. The four layers underneath it — where the
price differences actually live, and where the failures come from — were
not on the page at all.

WHAT THIS ADDS

A five-layer CSS-3D stack that pulls apart on scroll, painted with the
REAL photographed grain crops already in public/textures, switchable
across all five species in the catalogue. 3,762 bytes of new indexable
markup on the busiest page of the site.

Each layer carries the question to put to whoever is quoting. That is
the commercial point rather than the decoration: a visitor who leaves
this section knowing to ask for a wear-layer thickness in millimetres
and a moisture reading with a date can tell two quotes apart — and the
quote that answers all five is ours. Nothing here is a claim about a
job, a customer or an address; it is how hardwood floors are built.

TWO SILENT DEFECTS FOUND BY READING THE RECEIVING CODE

Neither throws, neither logs, and both look correct in review.

  1. /design matches its species param against FloorConfigurator's
     SPECIES list, whose `id` is the product's RATE KEY ('white oak'),
     not its catalogue id ('white-oak'). A link built from the id is
     dropped by the SPECIES.some(...) guard with no error, and the
     visitor lands on a configurator showing the default floor as
     though they had chosen it.
  2. /estimate reads a configuration through designConfigFromParams,
     which returns null unless species AND a positive sqft are both
     present. A link straight there would have carried a species the
     form discards on arrival — the same failure MEAS-04 fixed for the
     spec sheet.

So the handoff goes to /design, keyed by rateKey, and the test asserts
the round-trip against the catalogue AND against the line in
FloorConfigurator that does the matching, so the assertion cannot
quietly become a fiction.

F-205 IS WHY THE MOVING PART CONTAINS NO TEXT

The last animated element on this page was the hero stat. It rendered
through CountUp, which published three times in the DOM, and ecowoods.ca
served `26026+ Years in Toronto` in its server HTML to every machine
that read it.

Every word here — heading, lede, five layer names, five descriptions,
five questions, all five species names — is static JSX. The animated
stack is five EMPTY divs inside aria-hidden. There is no text path
through the animation, so there is no value it can publish twice. This
was verified by server-rendering the real component and counting: every
string appears exactly once in the served HTML, all five fa-layer
elements are empty, and all five species names are present whichever
one is selected.

P0.4 IS WHY THIS IS NOT THE HERO

The work this is modelled on runs WebGL above the fold. The hero here is
deliberately one static image with fetchpriority="high" because it is
the LCP element, and the ranking this page exists to win is worth more
than the animation. This sits below the published bands, where it costs
the LCP nothing.

NO ANIMATION LIBRARY, AND THE REASON IS CORRECTNESS

framer-motion is declared in package.json with no importers and stays
that way. A declarative motion library server-renders its INITIAL state
into the style attribute, so a scroll-triggered reveal ships the
COLLAPSED stack in the HTML and anything that does not run JavaScript —
which includes several of the answer engines this site is written for —
sees a flat rectangle.

Here the exploded, annotated, readable state is the CSS default, held by
`var(--fa-t, 1)`. It is what renders with no JavaScript and under
prefers-reduced-motion. Script only takes it away for a moment, below
the fold, so it can be given back as movement — and refuses to even do
that if the section is already on screen, which would be a flash of the
wrong state in front of somebody looking straight at it. The homepage
carries no new bytes for the animation.

VERIFICATION

  65 of 66 guards pass. verify:css cannot run in this sandbox (postcss
  unresolvable, npm registry 403) and fails identically on a clean
  checkout — so the stylesheet was instead parsed by Chromium's CSSOM:
  2,455 rules, 40 .fa- selectors, zero 404s, and layer 0's computed
  transform is translateZ(190px), proving the no-JS fallback resolves.

  78 assertions in floor-assembly.test.ts, each one mutation-tested:
  text inside a layer, a dropped --fa-t fallback, an id-keyed handoff
  and a species without a crop were each introduced and each caught.

  parse-scan clean on 625 files. Resolving typecheck clean.

